      "section": "methods",
      "text": "These emerging contaminants such as microplastics, nanoplastics, per - and poly - fluoroalkyl substances (PFAS), pesticides, and residual pharmaceutical products require new purification methods [1,2] . Among these, PFAS has got tremendous attention due to their severe effect on both nature and living creatures. The general formula of PFAS is C n F 2n + 1 R, where n represents number of carbons in the chain, F represents fluorine atoms, and R corresponds to a functional group. These contaminants are chemically stable, are hydrophobic, oleophobic, and they are resistant against biodegradation. As such they are persistent in the environment and also have a high half-life [3] . PFAS concentrations in natural waters span several orders of magnitude depending on proximity to emission sources. Monitoring studies commonly report individual PFAS in surface and groundwater at low-ng/L level (1 \u2013 10 ng/L) in background or rural areas, whereas substantially higher concentrations, occasionally reach - ing the \u03bc g L \u0000 1 range, have been observed at heavily impacted locations such as industrial sites. Epidemiological and toxicological studies have associated long-term exposure to certain PFAS with outcomes such as dyslipidemia, liver-enzyme alterations, and increased risks of kidney and testicular cancers, which has prompted progressively stricter regu - latory guideline values globally [4] ."
